[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I checked the OSPF “invisible bridge” (virtual link) that stitches the two halves together and it is currently healthy. The virtual link is configured between r4 and r8 over Area 1:
- r4 config: “area 1 virtual-link 18.1.4.1”
- r8 config: “area 1 virtual-link 14.1.4.1”

On both endpoints the virtual-link adjacency is up and Full:
- r4: “Neighbor 18.1.4.1 Full/DROther via VLINK0”
- r8: “Neighbor 14.1.4.1 Full/DROther via VLINK0”

This means the OSPF backbone continuity provided by the virtual link is intact. End-to-end reachability across the network also looks good: pc0 (20.1.1.10) and pc12 (120.1.1.10) ping each other with 0% loss in both directions.

Mechanism: If a virtual link endpoint restarts or its router-id changes, the vlink neighbor relationship drops and the non-backbone areas beyond it can appear “off the map” until the adjacency reforms. The logs on r8 show OSPF neighbors renegotiated after the recent restart, but they are fully up now.

Localization: OSPF on r4 and r8, interface VLINK0 on each.

Root cause: No ongoing fault; any outage was transient during a device restart while the virtual link re-established adjacency.

Corrective fix: None required at present. To prevent future flap-induced loss, ensure stable OSPF router-ids on r4/r8 and avoid making the transit area (Area 1) stub. If instability recurs, enable logging/monitoring for VLINK0 adjacency state. 
=== END ===